# Instrumented test discovery

Sibling of [android-app](android-app.mdc) and [ci-cost](ci-cost.mdc). Getting the
build onto the phone in the first place is the
[android-deploy](../../skills/android-deploy/SKILL.md) skill.

`connectedAndroidTest` is where the guarantees live that a JVM test cannot reach:
Room migrations, real SQLite semantics, DAO behaviour. It is also the task most
likely to tell you it verified them when it verified nothing.

This is the Android sibling of
[suite-discovery-guard](../playwright/suite-discovery-guard.mdc). There the suite
*shrank*; here it can be **entirely absent**, which is worse and harder to see.

## The bug shape

A repo's own notes said, as a hard rule:

> No `fallbackToDestructiveMigration()`, ever. Every version gets a real
> `Migration` **and a migration test that asserts the bin survives**.

and, under how to verify a change:

```powershell
.\gradlew.bat :app:connectedAndroidTest    # Room migrations, needs a device
```

There was no `app/src/androidTest` directory. Not an empty one — none. The
migration tests promised by the standard had never been written, and the command
documented as proving them printed:

```
> Task :app:connectedDebugAndroidTest
BUILD SUCCESSFUL in 4s
```

No warning, no `NO-SOURCE`, no result file. Two sibling repos by the same author
were checked the same way and were in the same state.

**Gradle protects the wrong case.** Verified on AGP 8.7.2 / Gradle 9.3.0:

| Situation | Result |
|---|---|
| Class filter names a class that doesn't exist | **exit 1** — `Failed loading specified test class 'X'` |
| `androidTest` source set absent entirely | **exit 0**, `BUILD SUCCESSFUL`, no result XML written |

So a *typo in a filter* fails loudly, while *having no tests at all* passes
silently. Exactly inverted from which one you would notice unaided.

The failure is not that tests broke. It is that a line in a standards document
("every version gets a migration test") was believed for the whole life of the
repo because the command that was supposed to enforce it always agreed.

## The guard

Do not read the exit code. Read the count.

A real run writes `app/build/outputs/androidTest-results/connected/**/TEST-*.xml`
whose root carries `tests=`, `failures=`, `skipped=`. A vacuous run writes
**nothing at all**, so "no XML" and "zero tests" are the same failure and one
check covers both.

```powershell
# Fail unless instrumented tests actually ran.
$dir = "app/build/outputs/androidTest-results/connected"
$xml = Get-ChildItem $dir -Recurse -Filter "TEST-*.xml" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
if (-not $xml) { throw "connectedAndroidTest produced no results: no androidTest source set?" }
$total = ($xml | ForEach-Object { [int]([xml](Get-Content $_.FullName)).testsuite.tests } |
          Measure-Object -Sum).Sum
if ($total -lt 1) { throw "connectedAndroidTest ran $total tests" }
"instrumented tests run: $total"
```

Wire the same assertion into CI if instrumented tests run there. A device-backed
job that silently runs nothing costs the minutes and buys none of the confidence.

**Check the count against intent, not just against zero.** Like the Playwright
case, the partial run — a filter that matches 3 of 13 — still exits 0 and still
writes a valid XML. Only your harness knows how many were meant to run.

## Running one class

Two things cost real time here and are in no error message.

**`--tests` is not valid for this task.** It is a JVM-test option; the connected
task rejects it outright:

```
Problem configuring task :app:connectedDebugAndroidTest from command line.
> Unknown command-line option '--tests'.
```

Use the instrumentation runner argument, with the **fully-qualified class name**:

```bash
./gradlew :app:connectedDebugAndroidTest \
  -Pandroid.testInstrumentationRunnerArguments.class=com.example.app.DaoArchiveTest
```

**In PowerShell that argument must be quoted.** Unquoted, PowerShell splits it on
the dot and Gradle reports a task nobody wrote:

```
Task '.testInstrumentationRunnerArguments.class=com.example.app.DaoArchiveTest'
not found in root project 'X' and its subprojects.
```

Nothing in that message mentions quoting, `-P`, or PowerShell, and the obvious
reading — that the task name is wrong — sends you to the wrong file.

```powershell
# Right: the whole -P argument is one quoted token.
.\gradlew.bat :app:connectedDebugAndroidTest `
  "-Pandroid.testInstrumentationRunnerArguments.class=com.example.app.DaoArchiveTest"
```

**The filter takes a class name, not a filename.** A file named `DatabaseTest.kt`
routinely declares `DaoArchiveTest` and `MigrationTest` and no class called
`DatabaseTest`; filtering on the filename yields `Failed loading specified test
class`. Read the `class` declarations, not the directory listing.

## Verify

- Delete or rename `src/androidTest` and confirm your guard fails. If the build
  still reports success, the guard is not wired in.
- After a real run, open the XML and confirm the specific test you just wrote is
  listed by name. A count alone cannot tell you *which* tests ran, and a new test
  that silently failed to register is the case you are guarding against.
